<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in GMT to MDT Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Confusing Mountain Standard Time (MST) with MDT</h3>
    <p>
      MST is UTC-7 (winter), whereas MDT is UTC-6 (summer). Assuming Denver is always UTC-7 leads to a 1-hour scheduling error during daylight saving months.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Ignoring Arizona's Non-DST Policy</h3>
    <p>
      Most of Arizona stays on MST (UTC-7) year-round and does not switch to MDT. If you schedule a call with Phoenix assuming it matches Denver during summer, your meeting will be 1 hour off.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Transatlantic DST Transition Discrepancies</h3>
    <p>
      The US switches to MDT on the second Sunday of March, while the UK switches to BST on the last Sunday of March. During these two weeks, the offset between London and Denver narrows to 5 hours.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Boundary Oversights on Evening GMT Calls</h3>
    <p>
      Converting early morning GMT times (e.g., 02:00 AM GMT) yields 8:00 PM MDT on the previous evening. Failing to note the date shift causes calendar invitation errors.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Software developers building global web applications require exact timezone database keys for proper offset calculations.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifier</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA database key for US Mountain Time is <code>America/Denver</code>. In Canada, <code>America/Edmonton</code> and <code>America/Calgary</code> are standard aliases.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset String</h3>
      <p>MDT is formatted in ISO 8601 strings as <code>-06:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T10:00:00-06:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for US Mountain Time is <code>MST7MDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0</code>, defining transition rules for March and November.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>System NTP Synchronization</h3>
      <p>NTP servers across Colorado and Utah synchronize against NIST atomic clocks located in Boulder, Colorado (WWV/WWVB).</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

